{"name":"Blue Batik","hex":"#6976a3","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"batik","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A wax-resist method of dyeing fabric."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dye fabric using the wax-resist method."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/batik"}],"entries":[{"title":"Batik","blocks":[{"text":"Batik is a dyeing technique using wax resist. The term is also used to describe patterned textiles created with that technique. Batik is made by drawing or stamping wax on a cloth to prevent colour absorption during the dyeing process. This creates a patterned negative when the wax is removed from the dyed cloth. Artisans may create intricate coloured patterns with multiple cycles of wax application and dyeing. Patterns and motifs vary widely even within countries. Some patterns hold symbolic significance and are used only in certain occasions, while others were created to satisfy market demand and fashion trends."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Batik_Trusmi_Cirebon_%2823%29.jpg/330px-Batik_Trusmi_Cirebon_%2823%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batik"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Niya_batik.jpg/250px-Niya_batik.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Niya_batik.jpg/500px-Niya_batik.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Niya_batik.jpg/250px-Niya_batik.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANiya_batik.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Egypt%2C_Byzantine_period%2C_1st_half_of_6th_century_-_Resist-Dyed_Hanging_with_Biblical_Scenes_-_1951.400_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-250px-Egypt%2C_Byzantine_period%2C_1st_half_of_6th_century_-_Resist-Dyed_Hanging_with_Biblical_Scenes_-_1951.400_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Egypt%2C_Byzantine_period%2C_1st_half_of_6th_century_-_Resist-Dyed_Hanging_with_Biblical_Scenes_-_1951.400_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Egypt%2C_Byzantine_period%2C_1st_half_of_6th_century_-_Resist-Dyed_Hanging_with_Biblical_Scenes_-_1951.400_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif.jpg?utm_source=